  "worked_solution": "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+5\\alpha =20\\,\u27f9\\,\\alpha =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4.\n$$",
